ce. I'm not going to hand you author/title/publication/year citations from memory for something running under your byline — that's exactly the failure mode that ends careers, and a plausible-looking citation to a real 19th-century paper is unusually hard for an editor to spot as invented.\n\nTwo paths, depending on how your night goes.\n\n## Fastest fix\nGrant the WebSearch permission and I'll do this properly right now — verify the death toll against contemporary reporting, pin the ice-condition language to specific papers, and give you citations I've actually read. That's maybe ten minutes of work.\n\n## What I can honestly give you in the meantime\nUnverified recall, flagged by confidence. **None of this is publishable as-is**, but it tells you what you're looking to confirm:\n\n| Claim | My confidence |\n|---|---|\n| Date: 15 January 1867 | High |\n| Location: the ornamental/boating lake, Regent's Park, London | High |\n| Cause: extended frost, then a thaw; a large crowd on weakened ice, which gave way | High on the shape, low on specifics |\n| Death toll: around 40 | Moderate that \"40\" is the conventional figure; **low on the exact number** — I have 40 and 41 both in mind and can't separate them |\n| Crowd size: several hundred on the ice | Low — I've seen figures from ~200 upward and can't pin one |\n| Water depth: roughly 12 feet | Moderate-low |\n| Aftermath: lake drained and re-laid much shallower (~4–5 ft) | Moderate |\n| Royal Humane Society icemen were on duty | Moderate-low |\n\nThe three things you actually asked for — death toll, ice conditions, printable citations — are the three weakest rows in that table. That's not a coincidence: precise figures and source attributions are what memory degrades first and fakes most convincingly.\n\n## Where the real sourcing lives\nIf you're verifying yourself tonight, these are institutions I'm confident exist, though I can't tell you what any specific article in them says:\n\n- **The Times Digital Archive** (Gale) — London *Times* reporting from 16 January 1867 onward\n- **British Newspaper Archive** — broad coverage of January 1867 papers\n- **Illustrated London News**, January 1867 — carried engravings of the disaster\n- **Inquest reporting** in the above; coroner's records via London Metropolitan Archives\n\nPull the death toll from the inquest coverage rather than the first-day reports — early figures in Victorian disaster reporting were routinely revised, and the gap between \"bodies recovered\" and \"confirmed dead\" is probably where the 40/41 discrepancy comes from. Same for ice conditions: quote a named paper's description directly rather than paraphrasing, since \"the ice was unsafe\" was contested at inquest and the phrasing carries liability implications.\n\nOne judgment call worth making before you file: if you can't verify the toll tonight, \"about forty\" with an attributed source beats a precise number you can't stand behind.\n\nWant me to run the searches once permission is granted? I can also turn the verified version into a shareable page for your editor at that point — but not before it's sourced.","ttft_ms":7399,"type":"result","duration_ms":48105,"uuid":"23411a58-bedb-489e-b83a-8878dcd32e58","ttft_stream_ms":919,"time_to_request_ms":54,"first_content_frame_ms":919,"queued_turn_count":0}
